# Security - Non-Negotiable

LawApp processes special category data (UK GDPR Article 9). Treat every control
as legally required (Article 32).

Never:
- Put secrets, credentials, keys, or tokens in code, compose files, or committed
  env files. Use the secrets manager or env references only. Never print a secret
  value, including in handoff or status output.
- Persist special category data for anonymous (unauthenticated) users. Anonymous
  tool runs are processed in memory and not stored.
- Build SQL by string concatenation. Parameterised queries only.
- Push to `main`, deploy to production Kubernetes, or rotate secrets.

Always:
- Enforce authentication and object-level authorisation on every endpoint. A user
  can access only their own matter, document, and deadline.
- Apply Postgres row-level security on tenant and matter.
- Pass user input and uploads through the redaction service before logging or
  embedding.
- Keep immutable logs (audit, brain traces) free of raw special category content;
  store metadata, decisions, and hashes so retention does not conflict with the
  right to erasure.

Flag for human review, do not auto-apply, any change touching: secrets, auth,
the verification gate, row-level security, a production-bound migration, or
network policy.
